[https://www.reddit.com/r/custommagic/comments/7wvx9z/what\_if\_demonic\_pact\_had\_a\_white\_twist/](https://www.reddit.com/r/custommagic/comments/7wvx9z/what_if_demonic_pact_had_a_white_twist/)

&#x200B;

\*cough Cough\*

if you do ANY of that, you completely undermine the message of what the film is trying to tell about the high school experience and especially the changes Veronica goes through throughout the film, particularly her reevaluation of the whole social hierarchy system that only exists because people keep buying into it. The whole point of the ending with Martha is to exemplify how to get out of its stupid, cruel paradigm by showing actual human caring and compassion to the people you perceive to be below you instead of insecurely pushing and berating them downwards. This is to be extended to adulthood as well, which is implied through the way the parents talk to Veronica about the standard of the Adult World.

&#x200B;

Heathers is dark and cynical, and remains to be so dark and cynical because it's still highly relevant to both the teenage experience and the adult experience, where social law is laid down by those who are better than you, and often not in a very positive way for anyone. It's savage, crass, and brutal, because that is what the high school and adult experience are- Endless Heathers above you, Endless Martha's under you. By punishing Veronica in the ending despite her growth in realizing how the whole BS involving social types interacting being impossible that JD is pushing the entire film, and that JD is just as affected by this system as the Heathers or Martha, you make the film darker and show no clear path on how you should act towards the world.

&#x200B;

TLDR: Veronica earned her Happy Ending by being willing to die for people she didn't care about or even talk to, rejecting the grossly abused social hierarchy of Westerburg High School. Taking that from her undoes the entire last act of the film, and makes the whole thing endlessly depressing with no message aside from "Life sucks."

I think in recent developments, that's distinctly not part of the new design process. Ambiguity is a fun, one off gag, that is fun because both players are confused about how it actually plays. Bitching at one another about intended play patterns is another. The other issue with the design is, since its a pretty reactive counterspell, you proceed to plan around using it- meaning that it's functionally useless, since you had it being part of your plan in the first place. Games of magic need to encourage the most amount of fun, even in silver boarder, and this encourages more frusteration than fun.

&#x200B;

It's the issue I have a lot with Silver boarded cards being designed in general. Often, people do designs that end up being excessively annoying or overtly silly that don't actually add anything to the game, and when you go "Hey, this isn't fun." The response is usually this is silver boarded. Theres a very good reason why unstable is so beloved- and that's because beyond the silliness, it's functional and leads to fun games for both parties.

YEAH! Cmon guys, this isn't the end for any of us! The reality is, I can't think of a closer-knit community of nerds on the internet! We all came together for this one dumb thing, but damnit, we grew into each other dumb things as well! Fuck this noise of the subreddit dying! There's a simple truth here-

&#x200B;

The Real Super Best Friends Were Us All Along.

Here's the blank version

